// pfodWeb NOTE: excludes GPIO15/34/36/39 (Wiegand card reader),
// GPIO17-19/21-23/25-27 (Ethernet EMAC/clock), GPIO32/35 (RS485) - see
// vendor pins_arduino.h. RELAY1-4/BUZZER stay kept.
//
// pfodWeb NOTE (2026-07-12): button/LED capability-tagging pass (section 7a
// of the board-variant-pin-audit playbook). GPIO5 (LED_BUILTIN in
// pins_arduino.h) and GPIO0 (RESET_KEY, a likely onboard reset/boot
// button) were both researched but left UNTAGGED - no change applied.
// "ViraLink-G0.1" exists upstream verbatim in espressif/arduino-esp32's own
// variants/ tree (this file matches the upstream pins_arduino.h exactly,
// confirming it is not a stale copy), and the vendor is a real company
// (ViraLink IoT platform / viralink.io / viralinkio on GitHub, an Iranian
// IoT-platform provider with public MQTT-client and gateway-firmware
// repos). However, no product page, schematic, datasheet, or teardown for
// the physical "G0.1" gateway board itself could be located after a
// genuine WebSearch/WebFetch attempt (GitHub org repo list, viralink.io,
// Farsi-language searches) - so neither GPIO5's LED polarity nor GPIO0's
// button behaviour (debounce, active level, or even confirmed physical
// presence vs. a software-only "key" concept) could be confirmed from real
// evidence. Per playbook policy, alias names alone (LED_BUILTIN,
// RESET_KEY) are not sufficient to tag - left as plain digital
// input/output pins, no capabilities array changes made. No bus-alias
// collision applies to either pin regardless (SDA=4, SCL=16, no SS/MOSI/
// MISO/SCK/TX/RX declared in this file) so this is not a contamination
// case, just an evidence gap. See
// boardsDetails/esp32/esp32/ViraLink-G0.1/notes.txt for the full source
// list and reasoning.
